## File 2: `src/errors/CustomError.js`

```javascript
/**
 * Custom Error Classes for the Book API
 *
 * WHY custom errors instead of plain Error objects?
 *   - They carry a `statusCode` so middleware can respond with the right HTTP code.
 *   - They are type-checkable (instanceof), letting us distinguish between
 *     "resource not found" and "bad input" without string-matching messages.
 *   - The `isOperational` flag separates expected business errors from
 *     unexpected system crashes, which is a pattern recommended by many
 *     Node.js production guides (e.g., Huo Siyuan's "Node Best Practices").
 */

/**
 * Base application error class.
 *
 * Extends the built-in Error so it works with `throw new AppError(...)`.
 * Captures three pieces of metadata:
 *   - statusCode  : HTTP response code (e.g., 400, 404, 500)
 *   - message     : Human-readable description
 *   - isOperational : true = expected error (don't alert on these),
 *                     false = unexpected bug (log and alert).
 */
class AppError extends Error {
  /**
   * @param {string} message - Description of the error.
   * @param {number} statusCode - HTTP status code to return.
   */
  constructor(message, statusCode) {
    super(message); // Pass message up to Error so .message works normally.

    this.statusCode = statusCode;
    this.isOperational = true; // Expected error, not a system crash.

    // Capture the stack trace at construction time (not where it's thrown).
    Error.captureStackTrace(this, this.constructor);
  }
}

/**
 * Thrown when a requested resource does not exist.
 * Maps to HTTP 404 Not Found.
 */
class NotFoundError extends AppError {
  /**
   * @param {string} [resource='Resource'] - Name of the missing resource (e.g., "Book").
   * @param {*} [id] - The ID that was requested but not found.
   */
  constructor(resource = 'Resource', id) {
    // Build a friendly message: "Book not found with id abc123"
    const message = id !== undefined
      ? `${resource} not found with id ${id}`
      : `${resource} not found`;

    super(message, 404);
  }
}

/**
 * Thrown when client input fails validation.
 * Maps to HTTP 422 Unprocessable Entity (or 400 Bad Request).
 */
class ValidationError extends AppError {
  /**
   * @param {string} message - Description of the validation failure.
   * @param {Object} [details] - Extra detail object (e.g., which field failed).
   */
  constructor(message, details = {}) {
    super(message, 422);
    this.details = details; // Attach extra context for the client to fix.
  }
}

module.exports = { AppError, NotFoundError, ValidationError };
```

**Key design decisions explained:**
- `AppError` is the parent so all custom errors share the same shape (`statusCode`, `isOperational`).
- `NotFoundError` auto-generates a message — less boilerplate in route handlers.
- `ValidationError` carries a `details` object so we can tell the client exactly which field failed (e.g., `{ title: "is required" }`).

